{"id": "v17-exceptions-rust-swallowed",
 "asserts": "`exceptions` on a Rust trace reports one line per Err CHAIN, at the chain's origin, and issues each chain the disposition its `chain.terminal` earns (design R8). A chain a written sink absorbed in a frame that then returned ok is the one shape reported as SWALLOWED, and it names the sink, the event and the frame; a chain a `#[test]` fn returned went back to the harness, and the mark comes from `meta.sites` -- the only place in a trace a `test`/`main` mark exists. Both chains print the whole journey they took, hop by hop, so a chain that crossed three frames by `?` is reported ONCE rather than three times. `terminal` is READ, never recomputed: strip it from an event and the verdict it carried disappears. And `meta.partial` -- the `?` sites the transformer could not reach -- is printed by `info` as a count and by `exceptions` as a header, because an Err raised at one of those sites is recorded by nothing and its absence from the answer says nothing about the program.",
